  "answer_markdown": "GreptimeDB was found by its historical name `greptimedb/greptimedb`: in the supplied catalog snapshot dated 2026-09-01, this alias is linked to [GreptimeTeam/greptimedb](https://github.com/GreptimeTeam/greptimedb). I conditionally recommend it for an initial evaluation; suitability for adoption has not yet been established. Its capability description, license, compatibility and server requirements are unknown. The latest commit and release dates are also unknown; `last_synced_at = 2026-08-11T13:41:45Z` denotes synchronization, not development activity. The supplied public-source reads dated 2026-10-11 concern other repositories and do not confirm GreptimeDB or update the catalog.\n\nGreptimeDB’s proposed role is a candidate database component for the prototype; specific functions require source verification. The possible connection “application interface → adapter → GreptimeDB” remains an architectural hypothesis. The team is responsible for the data contract, adapter, result verification and dependency maintenance. The expected outcome of the first evaluation is to determine whether the candidate warrants a separate trial. For the same set of operations, GreptimeDB, the existing or native mechanism and a custom implementation should be compared, accounting for integration and maintenance. The current mechanism and workload are unknown, so benefits and savings have not been established; complexity will depend on the interface, platform, dependencies, license and operational requirements.\n\nThe sole planning scenario is a hypothetical first-validation budget: 4–8 engineer-hours with low confidence, one engineer familiar with the target application and its component interface, at 8 hours per engineer-day and an allocated capacity of one engineer. This is neither a measurement nor a delivery commitment. Before any integration, the workload, existing path, language and platform, chosen version and license must be confirmed. The validation boundary